---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

/**
 * Note on wording: the original copy described Too Hard Basket as recording
 * "which recommendations were accepted, which were rejected". It does not —
 * per-recommendation positions are the coverage build, which is not published.
 * Claiming it here would be the exact failure the register exists to expose in
 * others, so the description below states what the register does today, and the
 * roadmap section states coverage as something still to come.
 */
const meta = [
  ["Status", "Live"],
  ["Site", "toohardbasket.org.au"],
  ["Data licence", "CC BY 4.0"],
  ["Code licence", "MIT"],
  ["Sources", "Every row links to its source in the official parliamentary record"],
];
---

<Base
  title="Projects"
  description="What APIA publishes: the datasets, their licences, and the sources behind them."
>
  <section class="wrap measure">
    <p class="eyebrow">Projects</p>
    <h1>What we publish</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      APIA publishes datasets, not opinion. Each project sets out its sources and its
      method, and the underlying data is available to anyone who wants to check it or
      use it.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page lists what APIA publishes — not every dataset within it. A single project
      may carry many datasets; those are listed on the project’s own site.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <article class="project">
      <p class="project-head">
        <span class="name">Too Hard Basket</span>
        <span class="badge">Live</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        When a parliamentary committee reports, government is expected to respond. Often
        it does. Sometimes it responds late. Sometimes it never responds at all, and
        nothing marks the fact.
      </p>
      <p>
        Too Hard Basket is a public record of what happens after the report: whether
        government responded, how long it took, and whether the response engaged with the
        committee’s recommendations or closed the file without answering them.
      </p>
      <dl class="facts">
        {meta.map(([k, v]) => (
          <div><dt>{k}</dt><dd>{v}</dd></div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap measure">
    <h2>What comes next</h2>
    <p>
      Too Hard Basket begins with one record — Senate committee reports awaiting a
      government response — and will extend to House of Representatives committees, to the
      state and territory parliaments, and to related records of the same kind: questions
      taken on notice, orders for the production of documents, statutory reviews that fall
      due and are not done, recommendations from royal commissions and the Auditor-General,
      and statutory deadlines that pass unmet.
    </p>
    <p>
      It will also go deeper rather than only wider. Recording that a response arrived is
      not the same as recording what it said, and the harder measure — for each
      recommendation a committee made, did the government state a position on it? — is the
      one that matters most. That work is under way and is not published yet. When it is,
      this page will say so.
    </p>
    <p>
      APIA’s job is to publish these and to keep publishing them. We would rather add one
      dataset a quarter and still be here in ten years than announce twenty and maintain
      none.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .project {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    padding: 1.25rem 1.4rem; background: var(--surface-sunken);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .project-head {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  .project .name { font-weight: 650; font-size: 1.0625rem; }
  .badge {
    font-size: 0.6875rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent); border: 1px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 2px; padding: 0.05rem 0.4rem;
  }
  .project dl.facts { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .project dl.facts > div:last-child { border-bottom: 0; padding-bottom: 0; }
</style>
